{
  "id": "ep232",
  "duration": 55.582,
  "beats": [
    {
      "start": 0.08,
      "end": 4.452,
      "text": "A billion-year-old star is glowing with elements that decay in decades.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Artist\u2019s impression of WD 0816-310, a magnetic white dwarf with a metal scar (eso2403a).tiff",
        "credit": "ESO/L. Cal\u00e7ada",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Artist%E2%80%99s_impression_of_WD_0816-310,_a_magnetic_white_dwarf_with_a_metal_scar_(eso2403a).tiff"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 2.767,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 2.767,
          "end": 4.467,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 4.452,
      "end": 9.947,
      "text": "Our Sun is mostly hydrogen, with heavy elements making up less than one percent.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Image of Sun From NASA\u2019s Solar Dynamics Observatory (PIA26681).tiff",
        "credit": "NASA/GSFC/Solar Dynamics Observatory",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Image_of_Sun_From_NASA%E2%80%99s_Solar_Dynamics_Observatory_(PIA26681).tiff"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 4.467,
          "end": 8.167,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 8.167,
          "end": 9.933,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 9.947,
      "end": 17.992,
      "text": "Located 356 light-years away in Centaurus, Przybylski's Star completely flips that solar recipe.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Eso2118d.jpg",
        "credit": "ESO, IAU and Sky & Telescope",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eso2118d.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 9.933,
          "end": 13.8,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 13.8,
          "end": 18.0,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 17.992,
      "end": 27.762,
      "text": "In 1961, astronomer Antoni Przybylski found rare-earth metals like holmium enriched by 1,000 times solar levels.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Antoni Przybylski SLNSW FL16054294.jpg",
        "credit": "unknown",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antoni_Przybylski_SLNSW_FL16054294.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 18.0,
          "end": 22.867,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 22.867,
          "end": 27.767,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 27.762,
      "end": 35.657,
      "text": "Later studies reported short-lived actinides like promethium, an unstable element that decays in under 18 years.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Periodic-Table.png",
        "credit": "Unknown author",
        "license": "CC0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Periodic-Table.png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 27.767,
          "end": 35.667,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 35.657,
      "end": 43.332,
      "text": "That sparked wild theories, including alien civilizations dumping radioactive nuclear waste into their sun.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "NASA-SETI-Sensitivity.jpg",
        "credit": "John H. Wolfe, Robert E. Edelson, John Billingham, R. Bruce Crow, Samuel Gulkis, Edward T. Olsen, Bernard M. Oliver, Allen M. Peterson, Charles L. Seeger, Jill C. Tarter.",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NASA-SETI-Sensitivity.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 35.667,
          "end": 43.333,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 43.332,
      "end": 50.602,
      "text": "However, a 2023 study showed overlapping spectral lines might explain away those short-lived elements.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Blue spectrum of HD126587 (noao0003a).jpg",
        "credit": "NOIRLab/NSF/AURA",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blue_spectrum_of_HD126587_(noao0003a).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 43.333,
          "end": 50.6,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 50.602,
      "end": 55.232,
      "text": "Yet the mystery of this slow-spinning cosmic anomaly remains unsolved.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Artist\u2019s impression of WD 0816-310, a magnetic white dwarf with a metal scar (eso2403a).tiff",
        "credit": "ESO/L. Cal\u00e7ada",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Artist%E2%80%99s_impression_of_WD_0816-310,_a_magnetic_white_dwarf_with_a_metal_scar_(eso2403a).tiff"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 50.6,
          "end": 55.567,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
